# Testing

Prefer a small set of meaningful **integration** tests over a large suite of unit tests.

## Prefer integration tests

- Cover real flows end-to-end (or nearly so): engine + wiring, UI behavior with the real stack, persist round-trips.
- Favor fewer, broader tests that would catch regressions a user would notice.
- Avoid one test file per function or exhaustive combinatorial unit coverage.

## Unit tests: pure only

Unit tests are reserved for **pure** logic that needs no mocks:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — pure function, real inputs/outputs, no mocks
expect(yearsToFreedom({ assets: 500_000, spend: 40_000, realReturn: 0.04 })).toBeCloseTo(18.2);

// ❌ BAD — unit test held up by mocks
const storage = { getItem: vi.fn(), setItem: vi.fn() };
vi.mock('./persist', () => ({ loadBaseline: () => storage.getItem('x') }));
```

- If the code under test needs mocks, stubs, or heavy fakes, write an integration test (or redesign for testability) instead of a mocked unit test.
- Do not mock collaborators to force a unit test; exercise the real boundary.
